select public.prepify_load_bank('Physics', 7, $j$[["Density is defined as:",["mass/volume","volume/mass","mass × volume","weight × volume"],"A",1,0,"ee9f12f3f8f7",1],["The SI unit of density is:",["kg/m²","kg/m³","N/m³","g"],"B",1,0,"35c79d552e88",1],["Temperature is related to the average:",["kinetic energy of particles","mass of an object","volume only","pressure only"],"A",1,0,"5cc0d5211066",1],["An increase in temperature generally increases an object's:",["internal energy","mass","density always","weight always"],"A",1,0,"114fe0552594",1],["A temperature scale provides:",["reference points for temperature measurement","only mass values","force values","density only"],"A",1,0,"be37f6c3c007",1],["A thermometric property is a physical property that:",["changes with temperature","never changes","depends only on mass","is always magnetic"],"A",1,0,"27b5a66ec345",1],["A liquid-in-glass thermometer commonly uses a liquid that:",["expands when heated","contracts when heated","has no thermal expansion","changes mass greatly"],"A",1,0,"11090e72a550",1],["A thermocouple works using the effect produced when:",["two different metals form a junction and temperature changes","a magnet is heated","a liquid boils","pressure becomes zero"],"A",3,0,"955153648088",1],["Sensitivity of a thermometer means:",["change in thermometric property per unit temperature change","maximum temperature only","minimum temperature only","size of bulb"],"A",3,0,"f471de081494",1],["Range of a thermometer is the:",["interval between its lower and upper limits","smallest division only","change in mass","accuracy only"],"A",1,0,"946265487927",1],["A linear thermometer has a thermometric property that changes:",["uniformly with temperature","randomly with temperature","only at one temperature","independently of temperature"],"A",3,0,"caecd4ae0121",1],["A high-sensitivity thermometer shows:",["a large change in reading for a small temperature change","no change in reading","only high temperatures","low range always"],"A",3,0,"cf2cedd31acb",1],["A thermometer with range −10 °C to 110 °C has a range of:",["100 °C","110 °C","120 °C","130 °C"],"C",3,0,"cd91fbb957e0",1],["A substance has mass 200 kg and volume 0.5 m³. Its density is:",["100 kg/m³","200 kg/m³","400 kg/m³","800 kg/m³"],"C",3,0,"a843b27c844d",1],["If a thermometer reading changes 6 units for a 3 °C rise, its sensitivity is:",["1 unit/°C","2 units/°C","3 units/°C","18 units/°C"],"B",3,0,"643c808807d0",1],["Which property can be used as a thermometric property?",["A property that changes with temperature","Object colour only","Mass of a fixed object","Name of the material"],"A",1,0,"5136a5c4f7e2",1],["The internal energy of a body is associated with:",["microscopic kinetic and potential energies","only its position","only its weight","only external work"],"A",3,0,"f39ab3be0e74",1],["Temperature is not the same as:",["internal energy","a measure related to particle motion","a physical quantity","a thermodynamic state variable"],"A",3,0,"58b9e2cd327a",1],["A thermometer should ideally have a thermometric property that is:",["measurably and predictably related to temperature","completely random","independent of temperature","always constant"],"A",1,0,"a5f73ff34c31",1],["A density of 1000 kg/m³ corresponds to:",["1 kg/L","10 kg/L","0.1 kg/L","100 kg/L"],"A",3,0,"79e6e84485cd",1],["A thermistor is placed in a temperature-controlled chamber. As the temperature changes, its electrical resistance changes significantly. The teacher explains that thermistors are particularly useful when a relatively large resistance change is required for a small temperature change. What characteristic makes them useful?",["High temperature sensitivity","Zero resistance","Constant resistance at all temperatures","Lack of response to temperature"],"A",3,1,"f3c100dd763a",1],["A thermometer is calibrated so that its liquid column is 2 cm at 0 °C and 12 cm at 100 °C. Assuming linearity, what temperature corresponds to a liquid-column length of 7 cm?",["25 °C","40 °C","50 °C","70 °C"],"C",5,1,"cf5862ae9eec",1],["A thermometer reads 10 divisions at 0 °C and 110 divisions at 100 °C. If its reading is 60 divisions, what temperature should it indicate, assuming a linear scale?",["40 °C","50 °C","60 °C","70 °C"],"B",5,1,"aa93197fbe54",1],["A balloon is filled with air and tied. When the balloon is squeezed gently, its volume changes significantly, unlike a solid metal block that hardly changes its volume under ordinary conditions. Which explanation best accounts for the behaviour of the gas?",["Gas particles are widely separated.","Gas particles are fixed in position.","Gas particles have no kinetic energy.","Gas particles cannot move."],"A",3,1,"74627469e462",1],["A rectangular metal block used in a school laboratory has a mass of 600 g. Its dimensions are measured as 10 cm × 5 cm × 3 cm. The students are asked to determine its density before identifying the material. What is the density?",["2 g/cm³","4 g/cm³","6 g/cm³","8 g/cm³"],"B",5,1,"cbfb6a0a17f8",1],["A student investigates why a small piece of iron sinks in water while a piece of wood of about the same size floats. The teacher explains that an object's behaviour in a liquid depends partly on how the object's density compares with the density of the liquid. Which statement is correct?",["Objects always float when their mass is large.","Objects float when their density is greater than that of the liquid.","Objects float when their density is less than that of the liquid.","Floating depends only on the object's volume."],"C",3,1,"fec650fddf8a",1],["A student heats a liquid in a container and notices that its volume increases. The teacher explains that this can be used to measure temperature because the volume changes in a predictable way over a suitable temperature range. What is this phenomenon called?",["Thermal expansion","Reflection","Refraction","Evaporation only"],"A",1,1,"29a7b330a095",1],["A resistance thermometer changes its resistance from 50 Ω to 70 Ω when the temperature increases by 40 °C. Assuming a linear response, what is the average resistance change per degree Celsius?",["0.2 Ω/°C","0.5 Ω/°C","2 Ω/°C","20 Ω/°C"],"B",5,1,"733c9009a86d",1],["A thermistor has a resistance of 2000 Ω at 20 °C and 1000 Ω at 40 °C. A student is studying the relationship between temperature and resistance. What is the change in resistance over this temperature interval?",["500 Ω","1000 Ω","1500 Ω","3000 Ω"],"B",3,1,"b1081a72a4c6",1],["A thermometer is calibrated so that equal increases in temperature produce equal increases in the liquid column over its working range. The teacher calls this a desirable feature because temperature differences can then be read directly from the scale. What is this property called?",["Linearity","Density","Expansion","Resolution of mass"],"A",3,1,"3fe3959cd559",1],["A resistance thermometer has a resistance of 100 Ω at 0 °C and 120 Ω at 100 °C, with an approximately linear response. What resistance would it have at 50 °C?",["105 Ω","110 Ω","115 Ω","120 Ω"],"B",5,1,"062f60184ae5",1]]$j$::jsonb, false);
select public.prepify_load_bank('Physics', 8, $j$[["A magnetic domain is:",["a group of atoms with parallel magnetic moments","a non-magnetic region","a current-carrying wire","a single electron"],"A",1,0,"8435b9419b75",1],["In an unmagnetized magnetic material, domains are generally:",["randomly oriented","all aligned","absent","stationary in one direction"],"A",1,0,"5c36c209c431",1],["In a magnetized material, domains are more:",["aligned","random","absent","heated"],"A",1,0,"438aa3b2ccba",1],["A bar magnet has:",["permanent magnetic properties","no poles","only one pole","no magnetic field"],"A",1,0,"da2467a95355",1],["A solenoid carrying current produces a magnetic field similar to that of:",["a bar magnet","a glass rod","a capacitor only","a resistor"],"A",1,0,"ce46ee849076",1],["The magnetic field inside a long solenoid is relatively:",["uniform","zero","random","circular only"],"A",3,0,"634592ca4dac",1],["Induced magnetism is produced when a magnetic material is:",["placed in a magnetic field","cooled in vacuum only","cut into pieces","insulated"],"A",1,0,"8628e1764fea",1],["Stroking a magnetic material repeatedly in one direction tends to:",["align its domains","destroy all domains","remove all atoms","increase temperature only"],"A",1,0,"c0feaa4b38e8",1],["Hammering a magnetic bar in a magnetic field can:",["align domains","always demagnetize it","remove its mass","stop all magnetic effects"],"A",3,0,"29fced40d89e",1],["Heating a magnet generally tends to:",["disturb domain alignment","increase permanent magnetism always","create poles in every material","have no effect"],"A",1,0,"4fc4f23fbb34",1],["Soft magnetic materials are materials that:",["magnetize and demagnetize easily","never magnetize","retain magnetism permanently only","are always non-magnetic"],"A",1,0,"090a120d7c44",1],["Hard magnetic materials are suitable for:",["permanent magnets","temporary electromagnets only","magnetic shielding only","liquid barometers"],"A",1,0,"bbcf6dcf9e60",1],["An electromagnet is a:",["temporary magnet produced by electric current","permanent magnet with no current","non-magnetic material","magnetic field line"],"A",1,0,"154574eeaa3f",1],["Magnetic shielding protects sensitive devices from:",["external magnetic fields","gravity","pressure","heat only"],"A",1,0,"29f5300ea2d5",1],["Magnetic tape records information by:",["aligning magnetic domains according to signals","melting the tape","changing its mass","using only light"],"A",3,0,"7291dde15929",1],["A loudspeaker converts electrical signals into:",["sound","light","pressure only","gravitational energy"],"A",1,0,"5f533669555b",1],["Earth's magnetic field is useful because it:",["helps protect life from cosmic radiation","eliminates gravity","creates weather","stops all radiation"],"A",1,0,"5e5e6178f408",1],["The magnetic and geographic north-south orientations of Earth are:",["opposite in orientation","identical in all respects","unrelated to each other","both absent"],"A",3,0,"5305a20fde94",1],["Magnetic field lines are closer together where the magnetic field is:",["stronger","weaker","zero","constant everywhere"],"A",1,0,"400640a01db9",1],["A solenoid has 100 turns per metre and carries 1 A. Using B = μ₀nI, its field is approximately:",["1.26 × 10⁻⁵ T","1.26 × 10⁻⁴ T","1.26 × 10⁻³ T","1.26 × 10⁻² T"],"B",5,0,"4bb10374b222",1],["A geography student marks the geographic North Pole on a globe and compares it with the position of Earth's magnetic pole. The teacher explains that the two are not at exactly the same place. Which statement is correct?",["Geographic and magnetic poles are exactly the same","Magnetic and geographic poles are different locations","Earth has only geographic poles","Earth has no magnetic poles"],"B",1,1,"2a9db992f634",1],["A compass is moved from a position close to a strong magnet to a position much farther away. The needle shows a much smaller deflection at the second position. What does this observation suggest?",["Magnetic field strength decreases with distance","Magnetic field strength increases with distance","Magnetic fields exist only inside magnets","A compass cannot detect magnetic field"],"A",1,1,"54adf7fe220c",1],["A steel paper clip is brought close to a powerful bar magnet. Although the clip was not a magnet, it can attract another small iron object while it is near the magnet. What has occurred?",["Permanent heating","Induced magnetism","Gravitational induction","Electrical charging"],"B",3,1,"5e873cb8569c",1],["A ship's navigation officer uses a magnetic compass to find direction. The needle lines up roughly north-south because Earth behaves in many ways like a giant magnet. Which statement is most accurate?",["Earth's magnetic field influences the compass needle","Earth's gravitational field turns the compass","The compass works because of atmospheric pressure","The compass works because of Earth's rotation alone"],"A",1,1,"9cf42fd28612",1],["In a laboratory investigation, iron filings are sprinkled on a sheet of paper placed over a bar magnet. After the paper is tapped gently, the filings arrange themselves in curved patterns around the magnet. These patterns are useful because they show:",["The mass of the magnet","The magnetic field pattern","The temperature of the magnet","The electric resistance of the magnet"],"B",1,1,"aaaf04b6a841",1],["A student examines two points around a bar magnet. At point P many magnetic field lines are crowded together, while at point Q they are widely spaced. Which statement is most reasonable?",["The field is stronger at Q","The field is stronger at P","The field has zero strength at P","Both fields must have identical strengths"],"B",3,1,"c7c6edee2f34",1],["A student compares two solenoids. Solenoid X has 200 turns and carries 3 A, while solenoid Y has 600 turns and carries 1 A. If the magnetic field strength is proportional to NI, which statement is correct?",["X has three times the field of Y","Y has three times the field of X","Both have the same field strength","X has half the field of Y"],"C",5,1,"817a759954fe",1],["A navigator travelling across different parts of Earth notices that a compass points along Earth's magnetic field rather than exactly towards the geographic North Pole. Which statement best explains this?",["Earth's magnetic poles and geographic poles do not exactly coincide","Earth has no magnetic field","The compass is controlled only by sunlight","Geographic poles are produced by magnets placed underground"],"A",3,1,"ba248e4e8d4c",1],["After experiments with bar magnets, solenoids, magnetic materials, compasses and electromagnets, a student concludes that magnetism is used in navigation, loudspeakers, magnetic recording and lifting heavy iron objects. Which statement best represents the overall importance of magnetism?",["Magnetism has applications only in laboratory experiments","Magnetism is useful in many technological, industrial and scientific applications","Magnetism is important only for permanent magnets","Magnetism has no practical applications outside physics laboratories"],"B",1,1,"34901b7097aa",1],["A student makes an electromagnet with 300 turns carrying 1.5 A. He then increases the number of turns to 600 while keeping the current at 1.5 A. If all else is unchanged and the field strength is proportional to the number of turns, what happens?",["Field strength becomes half","Field strength doubles","Field strength becomes one-fourth","Field strength remains the same"],"B",3,1,"0a9c8c433506",1]]$j$::jsonb, false);
select public.prepify_load_bank('Physics', 9, $j$[["Physics is the study of:",["matter, energy, space, time and their interactions","living organisms only","plants only","chemical reactions only"],"A",1,0,"4b62097389bb",1],["Which of these is a field of physics?",["Optics","Botany","Zoology","Genetics"],"A",1,0,"ecba98090835",1],["The study of heat, temperature and energy transfer is:",["thermal physics","astrophysics","geophysics","acoustics"],"A",1,0,"e78311715d4c",1],["The study of electric and magnetic forces is:",["electromagnetism","optics","acoustics","geophysics"],"A",1,0,"258aa27cabe9",1],["The study of microscopic particles is mainly:",["quantum mechanics","classical mechanics","nuclear physics","optics"],"A",1,0,"a06ab89fe21f",1],["The study of sound is:",["acoustics","optics","nuclear physics","cosmology"],"A",1,0,"540c8ba48e11",1],["The study of the motion of large (macroscopic) objects under forces is:",["classical mechanics","geomechanics","climate physics","optics"],"A",1,0,"a27a0264451b",1],["The study of the motion of microscopic particles under forces is:",["classical mechanics","quantum mechanics","nuclear mechanics","optics"],"B",3,0,"79f9839043a6",1],["The physics of the Earth is studied in:",["geophysics","astrophysics","biophysics","optics"],"A",1,0,"812c7440c05d",1],["The physics of weather and climate is studied in:",["climate physics","nuclear physics","optics","particle physics"],"A",1,0,"6070d33e0a36",1],["A hypothesis is best described as:",["a testable proposed explanation","a final law","a unit of measurement","a proven fact"],"A",1,0,"1ceda65e1af1",1],["A scientific theory is:",["a comprehensive explanation supported by evidence","a random guess","a unit","a single observation"],"A",1,0,"293df9a58241",1],["A scientific law generally:",["describes a consistent relationship or phenomenon","explains every mechanism in detail","is a guess","cannot be tested"],"A",3,0,"c941102ea117",1],["Which statement best distinguishes a theory from a law?",["A law describes what happens; a theory explains why or how","A theory is always less tested","A law is only a guess","They are always the same"],"A",3,0,"3c111972a41a",1],["A valid theory should:",["agree with experimental evidence","avoid testing","make no predictions","be logically inconsistent"],"A",1,0,"106bbc9a6fdd",1],["Falsifiability means a scientific statement:",["can, in principle, be disproven by evidence","is based only on opinion","is always true","makes no predictions"],"A",3,0,"04441cc737f2",1],["Scientists who specialize in physics are called:",["physicists","chemists","biologists","geologists"],"A",1,0,"f489c36d5145",1],["Physics is a branch of:",["the physical and natural sciences","mathematics only","biology only","engineering only"],"A",1,0,"5effee4c588d",1],["Which of these is a theory rather than a law?",["Theory of general relativity","Newton's first law","Pascal's law","Boyle's law"],"A",3,0,"dfb4050216e3",1],["Which of these is a law rather than a theory?",["Newton's laws of motion","Kinetic molecular theory","Bohr's atomic theory","Theory of general relativity"],"A",3,0,"12d4d07691d8",1],["A student thinks that increasing the temperature of a gas will increase its pressure when the volume is kept constant. She designs an experiment in which the volume stays the same while the temperature and pressure are measured. Why is her statement considered a hypothesis?",["It is an idea that can be tested experimentally","It has already become a scientific law","It cannot be tested","It is based only on mathematical calculation"],"A",3,1,"c1cceebb8efb",1],["A group of engineers is asked to design a bridge that can safely carry heavy vehicles. They use knowledge of forces, materials, equilibrium and energy, but must also consider cost, safety, environmental conditions and construction methods. What is the main role of engineering in this situation?",["Discovering only new scientific laws","Applying scientific knowledge to design and solve problems","Memorizing physical constants","Studying only astronomical objects"],"B",1,1,"8ec1c1c740f2",1],["A teacher demonstrates that whenever a certain set of physical conditions is kept, the same relationship between physical quantities is observed. The relationship has been tested many times and can be used to predict the behaviour of physical systems. This type of scientific relationship is best described as a:",["scientific law","hypothesis","personal opinion","scientific guess"],"A",3,1,"95a2f7a595a2",1],["A team of scientists observes a natural phenomenon, collects measurable evidence, develops possible explanations, performs controlled experiments, analyses the results and changes its explanation when the evidence does not support the original idea. Which characteristic of science is most clearly shown?",["Science depends on evidence and systematic investigation","Science depends mainly on personal beliefs","Scientific explanations cannot change","Scientists avoid experimental testing"],"A",1,1,"1a90956b3200",1],["A student notices that a metal spoon becomes hot when placed in hot water. She proposes that metals transfer thermal energy more rapidly than some other materials, and then tests her idea with spoons made of different materials. Her proposed explanation is best described as a:",["law","theory","hypothesis","measurement"],"C",3,1,"4d845eff48da",1],["A scientist investigating electrons inside an atom finds that their behaviour cannot always be explained by classical physics. She studies discrete energy levels and the probability of finding an electron in a particular state. Which branch of physics is most relevant?",["Quantum physics","Classical physics","Cosmology","Geophysics"],"A",3,1,"1903fb0ccc1e",1],["A student notices that a magnifying glass makes small objects appear larger. He investigates how light bends when it passes through the glass and how the lens affects the image. Which field of physics is mainly concerned?",["Quantum physics","Optics","Cosmology","Particle physics"],"B",1,1,"2606632537fc",1],["A group of scientists investigates the origin, evolution, large-scale structure and possible future of the universe. They study evidence from galaxies, the expansion of the universe and the early stages of cosmic development. Which branch of science is most directly concerned?",["Cosmology","Optics","Thermal physics","Biophysics"],"A",1,1,"fd9eea4b3a59",1],["A student observes the Moon, planets, stars and other objects in the night sky with a telescope. Her project focuses on identifying celestial objects, studying their positions and understanding their movements. Which field is most closely related to her project?",["Astronomy","Thermal physics","Particle physics","Biophysics"],"A",1,1,"7f42d554ee0f",1],["At a science exhibition, students investigate why a moving bicycle eventually stops when the rider stops pedalling. They measure its speed at different times and observe the effects of friction and air resistance. Their teacher explains that physics tries to understand natural phenomena by finding relationships between physical quantities and using them to make predictions. What main objective of physics does this show?",["To memorize scientific facts","To classify living organisms","To manufacture expensive machines","To understand and explain natural phenomena and their properties"],"D",1,1,"54debe023d29",1]]$j$::jsonb, false);
